# Copy to .env.local and fill in real values. Never commit .env.local —
# .gitignore already excludes it.
#
# THIS FILE IS COMMITTED. Every value below must stay a placeholder: it is
# the one file in the repository that is *supposed* to look like a real
# environment, which is exactly why a real value pasted here reaches
# GitHub unnoticed. (This has already happened once — see the security
# audit; the service-role key that was here must be treated as burned and
# rotated in the Supabase dashboard.)

# Supabase project settings → API. Safe to expose to the browser: the anon
# key has no power RLS doesn't already grant it (src/lib/env.ts, src/server/supabase/client.ts).
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-publishable-anon-key

# The one origin this product publishes about itself. Everything derives from
# it: email-confirmation and password-reset links (src/server/auth/actions.ts),
# canonical/OpenGraph URLs (src/app/layout.tsx), robots.txt and sitemap.xml.
#
# LOCAL DEVELOPMENT: http://localhost:3000, as below. This is also the built-in
# default, so a fresh clone runs without setting it.
#
# PRODUCTION AND PREVIEW DEPLOYMENTS: must be the real https:// origin, e.g.
# https://app.yourdomain.com. There is no correct default and none is guessed —
# supply it in the Vercel project's environment variables.
#
#   * IT MUST BE SET AT BUILD TIME. Every NEXT_PUBLIC_* value is inlined into
#     the bundle when `next build` runs, so setting it afterwards and
#     restarting changes nothing — you must REDEPLOY. Because of that, the
#     check in src/lib/env.ts runs at module load and fails the BUILD, which
#     is the last point where the mistake is still cheap.
#   * A deployment (VERCEL_ENV=production or preview) refuses to build if this
#     is localhost/127.0.0.1/::1/*.local, or if it is not https. Local
#     `npm run build` and the E2E suite are unaffected — they set no
#     VERCEL_ENV, and localhost is correct for them.
#   * The same origin must ALSO be added in Supabase, under
#     Authentication → URL Configuration: as the Site URL, and with
#     <origin>/auth/callback and <origin>/reset-password listed under
#     Redirect URLs. Supabase rejects any redirect target not on that
#     allowlist, so getting this variable right is necessary but not
#     sufficient — both sides have to agree.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Supabase project settings → API → service_role. SERVER-ONLY — bypasses RLS
# entirely (src/server/supabase/admin.ts), i.e. it can read and write every
# organization's financial data. Never expose to the client, never paste a
# real value into this file.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=your-service-role-secret

# console.anthropic.com API key. Server-only (src/domain/ai/providers/anthropic.ts).
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=your-anthropic-api-key

# Optional: direct Postgres connection string, used only by local RLS test
# tooling if you point it at a real database instead of the in-process
# PGlite harness in tests/rls/harness.ts. Not read by the app at runtime.
DATABASE_URL=

# Optional. Pepper for the rate limiter's identifier hashing
# (src/server/security/rate-limit.ts). The limiter works without it — it falls
# back to deriving the pepper from SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY. Set it (any
# random string of 16+ characters) so that rotating the service-role key does
# not reset in-flight rate-limit windows.
RATE_LIMIT_HASH_SECRET=

# ── Stripe (billing) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ALL SERVER-ONLY. None of these may ever be prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_ — that
# would inline them into the browser bundle. The browser never sees a Stripe
# key or a price id; it names a PLAN and the server resolves the rest
# (src/server/billing/stripe-config.ts).
#
# Leave every one of them EMPTY to run without billing: the pricing page then
# says "Billing setup required" and Checkout refuses, which is the honest
# state rather than a broken purchase. Setting SOME but not all is refused at
# startup — a deployment that can take payments but cannot hear about them
# leaves customers charged and un-upgraded.
#
# Use TEST-MODE keys (sk_test_…) until the product is genuinely live.
#
# Stripe Dashboard → Developers → API keys → Secret key.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
# Stripe Dashboard → Developers → Webhooks → your endpoint → Signing secret
# (whsec_…). Locally, `stripe listen` prints its own — see README.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# Stripe Dashboard → Product catalogue → your product → Price → API ID
# (price_…). One recurring monthly price per paid plan. These must match the
# $19 and $49 in PLAN_ENTITLEMENTS: Stripe charges the card, the canonical
# model decides what the customer gets, and nothing reconciles them
# automatically.
STRIPE_PREMIUM_PRICE_ID=
STRIPE_BUSINESS_PRICE_ID=

# ── Email ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ALL SERVER-ONLY. Never prefix any of these NEXT_PUBLIC_.
#
# Leave EVERY one empty to run without email: invoices can still be created,
# sent (marked sent, with a shareable customer link) and paid, and the UI says
# plainly that no email left the server. Setting SOME but not all is refused
# at startup — a deployment that can address mail but has no credential fails
# at the worst possible moment, mid-send to a real customer.
#
# EMAIL_PROVIDER=console is the development default: it renders the message to
# the server log, delivers nothing, and reports `deliversForReal: false` so the
# product never claims a customer received something.
#
#   console — logs only. No account anywhere required.
#   resend  — real delivery over plain fetch (no SDK dependency).
EMAIL_PROVIDER=console
# The From address. Must be a domain verified with the provider, or every
# send is rejected.
EMAIL_FROM_ADDRESS=
EMAIL_FROM_NAME=Countorra
# Optional. Where customer replies go — usually a real human inbox.
EMAIL_REPLY_TO=
# Required only when EMAIL_PROVIDER=resend. resend.com → API Keys.
RESEND_API_KEY=

# ── Plaid (bank connections) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# ALL SERVER-ONLY. None of these may ever be prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_: that would
# inline a bank-data credential into the browser bundle. The browser receives
# only a short-lived Link token, created server-side
# (src/server/bank-connections/providers/plaid).
#
# Leave every one EMPTY to run without bank connections: the Bank connections
# page then says no provider is configured, and nothing is imported. Setting
# SOME but not all is refused at startup (src/lib/env.ts) — a deployment that
# can start a bank Link but cannot store the resulting access token fails on a
# customer who has already typed their bank password.
#
# dashboard.plaid.com → Developers → Keys.
PLAID_CLIENT_ID=
# The secret for the environment named below. Sandbox and production have
# DIFFERENT secrets for the same client id.
PLAID_SECRET=
# sandbox | production. Never guessed, and never defaulted: this is the switch
# that keeps test runs away from real bank data. Use sandbox until the product
# is genuinely live, and keep production credentials out of every test.
PLAID_ENV=
# Where Plaid posts webhooks: the deployed https origin plus
# /api/bank-connections/webhooks/plaid. Leave empty in local development —
# Plaid cannot reach localhost, so no webhook is requested and manual refresh
# still works.
PLAID_WEBHOOK_URL=
# Only needed for OAuth institutions (many large banks). Must be registered in
# the Plaid dashboard EXACTLY, including scheme and path:
# https://app.yourdomain.com/app/bank-connections/oauth
PLAID_REDIRECT_URI=

# The shared secret a scheduler presents to the background sync endpoint
# (POST/GET /api/bank-connections/worker). REQUIRED for automatic imports:
# without it that route answers 404 and queued syncs, retries and continuations
# wait for somebody to press refresh. See BANK-SYNC-WORKER.md.
#
#   Generate:  openssl rand -base64 32
#   Send as:   Authorization: Bearer <this value>
#
# On Vercel Cron, set this to the same value as CRON_SECRET — Vercel sends that
# as the bearer token. Never prefix it with NEXT_PUBLIC_.
BANK_SYNC_WORKER_SECRET=

# What Vercel Cron sends as its bearer token (vercel.json runs the worker every
# five minutes). On Vercel it MUST be the same value as BANK_SYNC_WORKER_SECRET
# — it is not a second key. A mismatch is reported as
# bank.worker_cron_secret_mismatch. Leave empty outside Vercel.
CRON_SECRET=

# Optional dead-man's switch: an https URL at a service that alerts when pings
# STOP (healthchecks.io, Cronitor, Better Stack…). Pinged after each successful
# worker invocation, with no body. Usually contains a secret path — never
# commit a real one, and it is never logged.
BANK_SYNC_HEARTBEAT_URL=

# Key material that encrypts provider access tokens at rest
# (src/server/bank-connections/credential-crypto.ts, table
# bank_provider_secrets). REQUIRED whenever Plaid is configured — a bank access
# token is never stored in the clear.
#
#   Generate:  openssl rand -base64 32
#   Format:    <key-id>:<base64 32 bytes>  — newest key FIRST, comma-separated.
#              A bare base64 key is accepted and treated as id "primary".
#   Name:      BANK_CREDENTIAL_ENCRYPTION_KEYS (plural) is accepted as an alias,
#              because the value is a list; the singular wins if both are set.
#   Rotate:    add the new key at the front, re-encrypt
#              (rotateStoredCredential), then drop the retired key.
#
# Losing this key means no stored bank credential can be decrypted: every
# connection must be linked again. It is not recoverable from the database,
# which is the point.
BANK_CREDENTIAL_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
